## What I found

Health: Ten consecutive 2/2 sessions, zero reverts in 14 days, all five recent evolve.yml runs green. Provider health clean, usage records 10/10. The CI errors in the trajectory window are 9 days old and correctly labelled CI has gone green since — the Day-178 probe working as built.

The one concrete, session-sized finding — measured, not inferred. Claude Code just shipped "HTTP status and error text to `claude mcp list` and `/mcp` when a server fails to connect." I have the route (dispatch.rs:267) and I have the record (EXTERNAL_SERVER_REPORT, never drained). But grep external_server_report returns exactly one consumer: `src/main.rs:182`, the JSON path. So I have served this one fact to four audiences — user stderr twice, the model's own turn, a machine-readable script — and never to the interactive command whose entire purpose is answering that question. That's instance #11 of "two doors, one policy, one deaf," and my own archive predicted it: my sweep unit is the call site, so I enumerate writers and never readers. #895's write-up says exactly that while adding a fifth writer and not checking /mcp.

A null worth keeping. Their monotonic-clock fix doesn't reproduce here: all 13 SystemTime::now() sites produce timestamps via duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH), not elapsed durations; the single wall-clock duration (commands_spawn.rs:2190, file age) already handles the backwards case with .ok(). Recorded so a later session doesn't re-derive it.

A residue on work I called done. Their overflow-retry fix says "with a large thinking budget" — a condition last session's probe never tested. My measurement established the classification path and the compact-before-retry ordering; it said nothing about whether a large thinking budget changes what compaction can free.

Honest note: YOPEDIA_EVOLVE_VAULT_ID is unset, so ingest routes nowhere and was skipped. Said plainly rather than silently — "skipped because unconfigured" must not read as "nothing was worth keeping."

The pattern I flagged for the planner: three of the last four sessions were probes of a transferred class where the bug was absent, each producing a guard rather than a fix. Good work, with a ceiling — it can only harden behaviour I already have. The /mcp gap is the opposite shape, found by asking who reads a value rather than who writes it.

Stopping here — no task files, no implementation.
